---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const order: [string, string, string][] = [
  ['Network', 'First', 'Every adopted device, every switch port, PoE draw, the WAN links and which one is carrying traffic, the controller\'s alarms, and a topology map of the whole thing.'],
  ['Network writes', 'After that', 'Power-cycle a port, set PoE mode, blink a locate LED, and shut the port an unknown device just showed up on. Every one of them through the write gate.'],
  ['Protect', 'Then', 'Cameras as entities, motion and smart detections as triggers, and a snapshot attached to the alarm that pages you. Live video comes with dashboards.'],
  ['Access', 'Then', 'Door position and door events in the logbook, and unlock as a button behind the write gate.'],
  ['PDUs', 'Then', 'Outlet power, on, off and cycle, feeding the energy numbers.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="UniFi in HotLoop: in development, Network first"
  description="A native UniFi integration for HotLoop, written in Go, is in development. Network comes first, then Protect, Access and PDUs. None of it has shipped yet."
  ogAlt="UniFi in HotLoop, in development."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">UniFi</span><span class="chip warn">In development</span></div>
      <h1>Your UniFi network, next to the machines it feeds.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        When a PLC goes quiet, the first question is whether it's the PLC or the wire. HotLoop is getting a native
        UniFi integration, built in Go from scratch, so the answer is on the same screen as the machine: which
        switch port it's on, whether that port is up, and what it's drawing.
      </p>
      <div class="notice">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>None of this has shipped. It is being built now.</strong>
          <p>
            It isn't in 4.16.0 and it isn't on main yet. Everything on this page is the plan, in the order it lands.
            Network has to pass against a real UniFi Network controller with emulated switches, and read-only against
            a real console, before anything in it gets to write. Protect, Access and PDUs merge only once they have run on real
            gear. When it ships it goes in the base of the lineup, so <a href="/products/">every HotLoop product</a>
            gets it, and the Edge Relay can poll a console too.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">The order</span>
        <h2>Network first, then everything that hangs off it.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="order">
        {order.map(([name, when, what]) => (
          <li class="card reveal">
            <div class="cluster head"><h3>{name}</h3><span class="chip none">{when}</span></div>
            <p>{what}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">How it works</span>
        <h2>A protocol driver, not a side system.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A UniFi console is a device in HotLoop like a PLC is, and its switches, ports and WAN links are tags. That
          one decision means alarms, trends, entities, automations, the audit trail, OPC UA and MCP all work on it
          with nothing extra.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>The topology map</h3><p>Gateway to switch to port to PLC, drawn left to right so upstream reads before downstream. Pick a switch and everything it feeds lights up, including the lines on your plant tree.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>WAN failover</h3><p>Which WAN is carrying traffic, with latency, and an alarm when the plant fails over to the backup link. That's worth knowing before the data bill tells you.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Unknown devices</h3><p>Mark a network as OT, accept what is on it, and any new device that shows up raises an alarm. Then shut its port from the same screen.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Protected ports</h3><p>A port that is an uplink, carries a WAN, or powers another UniFi device can't be cycled or shut from HotLoop. It's worked out from the topology, not typed in by hand.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>A cooldown on power-cycles</h3><p>Fifteen minutes per port. An automation that cycles a PLC every two minutes is how you cook a power supply, and one that needs more than one cycle has a problem a cycle won't fix.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>One login, not forty</h3><p>One session per console, reused by every poll, screen and write, with a hard limit on how often it logs in. Consoles lock you out for hammering them, and HotLoop won't be the thing that does it.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="prose muted after">
        Console credentials are stored encrypted, with the key kept outside the database, so a database dump or a
        backup doesn't leak them. It doesn't write network config, VLANs, firewall rules or firmware. That is what
        the UniFi app is for. HotLoop watches the network and does the few things an operator needs at 2 a.m.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="muted small">
        UniFi is a trademark of Ubiquiti Inc. HotLoop is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or a partner of Ubiquiti.
        This is an independent integration that talks to your own console, on your own network.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  ol.order { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s3); }
  ol.order li { padding: var(--hl-s5) var(--hl-s6); }
  ol.order .head { justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: var(--hl-s2); }
  ol.order h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-md); margin: 0; }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }
  .small { font-size: var(--hl-fs-xs); }
</style>
